Vulnerability Title

Stored HTML Injection in Sandbox Model Name Field

Risk Level

Low

CVSS v3.1 Score (Intranet Application)

3.5 – Low

Vector String:
CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N

Vulnerability Category

Improper Input Validation / HTML Injection

OWASP Category

OWASP Top 10 2021 – A03: Injection
https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

Vulnerability Description

The application provides a Sandbox module where users can create new sandbox models and specify a model name. During testing, it was observed that the application does not properly sanitize or validate user input in the model name field.

When the following HTML payload was entered as the model name:

<a href="https://www.google.com" target="_blank">Click Me</a>

the application stored and rendered the HTML code directly in the user interface. As a result, a clickable hyperlink was created within the application. When the link was clicked, the user was redirected to the external website (google.com).

This behavior indicates that the application allows HTML injection in user-controlled input fields, which could potentially be abused to inject malicious links or content.

Impact

Attackers may inject clickable external links within the application interface.

Could be used to redirect users to malicious or phishing websites.

May impact user trust and application integrity.

If combined with additional payloads, this vulnerability may lead to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) in certain scenarios.

Proof of Concept (PoC) Steps

Step 1: Login to the application with valid credentials.

Step 2: Navigate to the Sandbox module.

Step 3: Click on Create New Sandbox Model.

Step 4: In the Model Name field, enter the following payload:

<a href="https://www.google.com" target="_blank">Click Me</a>

Step 5: Save the sandbox model.

Step 6: Observe that the application renders a clickable link labeled “Click Me”.

Step 7: Click the link and confirm that the application redirects the user to google.com.

Recommendations

Implement server-side input validation and sanitization for all user input fields.

Encode or escape HTML characters before displaying user input in the UI.

Use output encoding mechanisms to prevent HTML rendering.

Implement Content Security Policy (CSP) to reduce the risk of malicious script execution.

Apply secure coding practices to prevent injection vulnerabilities.

